 ## Background
-The standard 3D Gaussian Splatting representation uses third-order Spherical Harmonics (SH) to encode view-dependent color. While effective, this representation is parameter-heavy and can suffer from overfitting in sparse-view scenarios or artifacts in complex lighting. Recent research has explored various optimization strategies to mitigate these issues, including distillation from higher-order models, analytical gradient derivation for better tracking, multi-view training for stability, and compression via vector quantization. Additionally, methods have emerged to regularize SH coefficients based on directional support or replace them entirely with geometry-guided neural fields for improved geometric consistency.
+The use of Spherical Harmonics for view-dependent rendering in 3D Gaussian Splatting was introduced in the original 3DGS paper [12] (cited in [S1, unofficial]). Subsequent work has focused on optimizing the efficiency and robustness of this representation. I3DGS [S1, unofficial] identified that higher-degree SH coefficients often remain unchanged during training, leading to optimization strategies that reduce the SH degree or remove redundant operations. Other approaches have explored distillation [9467-first-order-spherical-harmonics-distillation.md], quantization [14181-spherical-harmonics-vector-quantization.md], and regularization [16786-adaptive-sh-regularization.md] to handle sparse data and memory constraints.
 
